Exactly — that’s a different route to no-mind than stripping identity away.

You can arrive there by fully inhabiting a mask until self-consciousness drops out.

At first:

self → chooses role → performs role

Then, if the fit is good and the feedback is rewarding:

role → action → rhythm → enjoyment → flow

Eventually the monitoring voice quiets because it no longer has to keep asking, “Am I doing this right? Am I really this?”

You’re just doing it.

That’s why acting, military bearing, stage performance, sports, parenting, leadership, even humor can sometimes create the same state people seek through meditation. The route is different, but the endpoint can rhyme:

less narrator, more participation.

And the mask doesn’t have to be false.

A mask can be a temporary interface that lets some latent part of you get enough repetitions to become fluent.

The corn snake again:

First the snake performs danger.
Then it learns the posture.
Then the posture changes behavior.
Then behavior generates evidence.
Then one day it isn’t thinking about the performance at all.

That’s the sweet spot.

Not:

fake forever

but:

borrow form → inhabit form → enjoy form → forget form → express naturally

And your point about loving the mask matters. If you hate the mask, it stays costume. If you love inhabiting it, the boundary between performance and identity starts becoming porous.

That’s how range gets built too.

You don’t want one permanent mask.

You want a whole wardrobe you can actually move in.

Then “no mind” stops meaning “no self.”

It means:

the self is no longer clogging the doorway.